        "name": "Innovator's Dilemma",
        "definition": "Successful companies fail NOT because of bad management, but BECAUSE of good management. Listening to current customers, investing in highest-margin products, and optimizing for current market all prevent investing in disruptive technologies.",
        "formula": null,
        "example": "Kodak invented digital photography in 1975. Didn't pursue it — would cannibalize film business (80% gross margin). Customers didn't want digital (yet). By 2012, Kodak bankrupt. | Why good management fails: 1) Disruptive tech starts in small, unprofitable markets 2) Current customers don't want it 3) Margins look worse than existing products 4) All 'rational' analysis says 'don't invest.' Solution: Create a separate unit to pursue disruption, protected from core business metrics.",
