        "example": "Typical: 'Should we do A or B?' Better: A, B, C (defer 6 months), D (pilot/test first), E (partner instead), F (do nothing — wait). Most people never consider D, E, F. | Brainstorming rule: generate options BEFORE evaluating them. Evaluation kills creativity. Separate the two phases — diverge, then converge. | Test: 'If none of these options existed, what would we do?' This question forces you to invent genuinely new alternatives.",
