        "id": "ed36c0c9-9d76-4fc3-873d-edb6b3c84bc8",
        "name": "Decision Batching & Cadence",
        "definition": "Group similar decisions into regular cadences (weekly, monthly, quarterly) instead of making them ad-hoc. Creates organizational rhythm, reduces context-switching, and makes decision-making a repeatable process.",
        "formula": null,
        "example": "Monday 9am: weekly tactical decisions (approvals, resource allocations). Thursday 2pm: weekly strategic review (pipeline, metrics, blockers). Monthly: resource reallocation, hiring priorities. Quarterly: strategy review, OKR setting, kill/accelerate decisions. | Jeff Bezos: no meetings before 10am. Takes his highest-IQ hours for strategic thinking. BATCHES meetings (and decisions they require) in the afternoon. | Decision cadence prevents constant interruptions. When teams know 'we decide pricing on Thursdays,' they prepare and the decision quality improves.",
