        "name": "Multi-Party Agenda Setting",
        "definition": "Control the sequence of negotiations: who gets approached first, which issues get discussed when, and what's the deadline. Early wins create momentum; early concessions create expectations of weakness. Frame the issue in a way that favors your coalition.",
        "formula": null,
        "example": "International climate negotiations: host country controls the agenda. They set the topic order (emissions first, finance second), decide who speaks when, and draft the initial text. Whoever writes the first draft controls 80% of the final outcome. | Corporate M&A with multiple bidders: you're the seller. Set the process — sealed bids by Friday, best-and-final offers only, no 'topping' after winner selected. Control the rules, control the outcome. | Internal: mediating between Sales and Engineering on resource allocation. Set the agenda: 'Let's first agree on WHAT we're trying to achieve, then discuss HOW.' The shared goal framing reduces positional negotiation.",
